{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1791311791,"nonce":"SEuB/S4pzhYBZOS+awlh/b/4dco=","message":"The broad cold-path dispatch-count experiment repairs sparsematmult: at standard counts its wall median is 568 -\u003e 550 ms and its warmed iterations stay around 52-56 ms. FFT is 1205 -\u003e 1172 ms. Two quiet NUC comparisons put SOR wall at 8130 -\u003e 6800 ms and 8107 -\u003e 6621 ms. Nineteen inliner tests and 43 Lua/50 Python LLVM conformance groups pass locally.\n\nLua full-suite/repeat reveals the scope is still too broad: binary_trees initially 199 -\u003e 209 ms, then 198 -\u003e 200 ms; op_overload 214 -\u003e 221 ms, then 209 -\u003e 215 ms; tokenize 195 -\u003e 200 ms, then 192 -\u003e 197 ms. Do not treat the first trees result as a stable 5% regression, but the smaller operator/tokenization increases repeat. Runtime inline traces have identical frontend inline sites: the library is optimized into its snapshot at build time, so runtime-only traces do not expose its changed internal decisions.\n\nA narrower follow-up keeps the original dispatch policy except for scalar helpers whose entire body is \u003c=24 instructions, with no loop or hot stores/allocations, at most one hot fallback, and a fallback result returned directly or through a single empty return join. Cold error paths still count toward all copied-body/caller budgets. A return-phi regression test covers the shape introduced by nested inlining; it also executes both branches before/after and checks retention of cold calls. This candidate is staged for measurement, not committed.\n\nBroad-cold patch saved locally as /tmp/zyntax-scalar-adapter-cold.patch. NUC results: /tmp/zyntax-nuc-scalar-adapter-cold-{speed-standard,speed-ab,speed-all,lua-repeat}.log and corresponding JSON (the Lua repeat reused the lua-ab JSON path; the original full JSON was copied locally first). Runtime traces: /tmp/zyntax-inline-{binary_trees,op_overload}-{pool-cursor,scalar-adapter,scalar-adapter-cold}.trace. No forced tiering; backend subcommands returned llvm for both candidate frontends.","files":null}]}